From 7e9eadef0e8dc796dfe84fb0320f9899cfb0a852 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pruned-electrs <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 06:16:20 -0500
Subject: [sparrow PATCH] Follow a chain whose block headers are not all 80
 bytes

The client half of docs/electrum-header-v2.md. Needs the drongo patch
beside it, which is what actually reads and hashes a v2 header; this is
the three places Sparrow had a literal 80 in it.

VariableHeaders holds all of it, so removing support later is a file
deletion plus reverting the call sites that name it.

  - blockchain.block.headers returns one concatenated hex string below
    protocol 1.6, and Sparrow negotiates below that, so it always
    receives that form and has to walk it. Three call sites sliced it on
    a fixed stride; on a chain spanning the activation a 40 header
    response is 4964 bytes, which a fixed stride reads as 62 headers of
    garbage. The walk reads each header's length from its own version
    field and fails if it does not land exactly on the end.

  - The on-disk store indexes by height times a stride, so the stride is
    now per network rather than per header and an 80 byte header is
    padded out where both can occur. Only testnet4 can, so every other
    network is byte-for-byte as before and existing mainnet stores are
    untouched. A store written with the other stride is already handled:
    one whose first record does not descend from the pin is discarded and
    re-downloaded, and a padded record cannot descend from anything.

  - SUPPORTED_VERSIONS reaches 1.8. A server on a chain with v2 headers
    refuses to negotiate below it, so this is what makes such a chain
    reachable at all; the range is a minimum and a maximum, so servers
    elsewhere still negotiate 1.4.2.

Sparrow's checkpoints are not in the way: testnet4 pins 74 periods, so
149183, which is 354 blocks below the activation and on shared history.

Blake2bChainSyncHarness drives the whole path against a live server and
is a main rather than a test, since it needs one. Against electrs on a
regtest chain activating at 20: negotiated 1.8, split 40 headers into 19
v1 and 21 v2, and HeaderChainState accepted all 40 under its linkage,
difficulty, proof-of-work and median-time-past rules. 195 Sparrow tests
still pass.
